While I understand and respect your argument, in my opinion this is a
_feature_ of NetHack, and not a limitation. Or rather, it is a
limitation, but the choice to limit oneself is part of artistic
expression. Like when a writer chooses to write sonnets, or an artist
chooses to paint in oils on canvas. What makes NetHack most appealing
to me is that it has deliberately limited itself to ASCII text in an
80x25 window, and yet has explored that limited medium and produced
something quite remarkable.

For this reason, I never play NetHack in tiles mode. I always go back
and play it in a standard full-screen 80x25 terminal. I'm not a total
purist. I accept colour, and IBMgraphics - and I'd love to see Ray's
Unicode nethack become a reality - but tiles are just not for me.

As a patch-writer, I've come up against these limitations from time to
time and  found it frustrating that I can't represent something the
way I want - but then this is also where I find I have to get most
creative. How can I convey the feature I want yet still maintain the
NetHack "feel"? I think this has been the source of a lot of the
richness of nethack, and I would rue the day when it was discarded.

